[FINAL-TEXT] I now have definitive confirmation of the root cause. The `ripd.conf` on router2 (and all routers) is **0 bytes** — empty — created at lab start (10:06 UTC) but never populated. The RIP configuration exists only in memory.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The root cause is an empty `/etc/frr/ripd.conf` on **router2** (confirmed: 0 bytes, created 10:06 UTC). All three routers share this structural defect, but router2 is the critical originator: it is the only device that injects the server-room prefix `1.0.0.0/8` into RIP. The startup script applies the RIP configuration (`network 1.0.0.0/8`, etc.) entirely via `vtysh` in-memory commands but never executes `write memory`, so nothing is persisted to `ripd.conf`.

When router2's `ripd` process was restarted at some point this afternoon — either via `watchfrr` after a daemon crash or a manual `frr restart` — it reloaded from the empty config file and came back up with zero configured networks. Router2 immediately ceased advertising `1.0.0.0/8`. Router1 heard no further updates for that prefix; after 180 seconds its RIP timeout fired, and it withdrew the route. Router0 followed suit 180 seconds later, removing its `R>* 1.0.0.0/8` entry. With no route to the server room on router0, every CS lab host (192.168.2.x) that tried to forward packets to 1.0.0.x got a "no route to host" and the server room effectively vanished for all of them simultaneously. The lab restart at 10:06 UTC re-ran the startup scripts, re-applied the vtysh RIP config, and restored connectivity — but the empty `ripd.conf` vulnerability remains.

Corrective fix: add `-c "write"` (or a separate `vtysh -c "write memory"` call) at the end of each router's startup vtysh sequence so that the RIP configuration is persisted to `ripd.conf`. This makes the config survive any future `ripd` restarts triggered by `watchfrr` without requiring a full lab restart.
=== END ===